  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   The central idea is to use a **Genetic Algorithm (GA)** with adaptive repair mechanisms to evolve candidate solutions for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Unlike local optimization methods, GA explores a broader solution space by combining crossover and mutation on a population of candidate vectors. This helps avoid local optima found by gradient-based methods. The algorithm repairs infeasible candidates using a clipping and adjustment method to enforce constraints, ensuring all solutions satisfy the sum and bound conditions. The rationale is that diverse representations (non-adjacent supports) may yield lower overlap, which local methods might miss.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   - **Variables**: Binary vectors (discretized step functions) of length \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\) with \\(n_{\\text{sum}} = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\) (integral constraint).  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))  dx\\), approximated by \\(\\max(\\text{full correlation}) \\times dx\\).  \n   - **Constraints**:  \n     \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\),  \n     \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\\text{points}}} h_i = n_{\\text{sum}}\\) (integral \\(\\int h  dx = 1\\)).  \n   - **Operations**:  \n     - Crossover: One-point crossover with probability \\(p_{\\text{cx}}\\).  \n     - Mutation: Bit-flip style mutation with probability \\(p_{\\text{mut}}\\).  \n     - Repair: Clip to [0,1] and adjust sum iteratively.  \n   - *(No optimization libraries are required; repair ensures constraints.)*\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   - **Input**: `seed`, `budget_s`, optional parent solution (handled by caller).  \n   - **Steps**:  \n     1. Initialize population (`pop_size=100`) with uniformly sampled vectors, ensuring sum \\(n_{\\text{sum}}\\) and bounds. Repair ensures feasibility.  \n     2. **Parent selection**: Tournament selection (tournament size 5).  \n     3. **Crossover**: Apply one-point crossover with probability 0.5; repair both children.  \n     4. **Mutation**: Flip random bits (within [0,1]) with probability 0.01; repair if invalid.  \n     5. **Repair function**: Clipping plus iterative sum adjustment (up to 500x, stopping if fixed).  \n     6. **Objective evaluation**: Compute \\(C_5 = \\max(\\text{full correlation}) \\times dx\\), rescaled to \\([0, 1]\\) for bit-width independence.  \n     7. **Termination**: Up to 500 generations or budget expiry.  \n     8. **Output**: Best \\(h\\) and \\(C_5 \\times 10^{10}\\) (scaled for code reflection).  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n   - **Default hyperparameters**:  \n     - Population size: 100  \n     - Crossover/mutation probs: \\(p_{\\text{cx}} = 0.5\\), \\(p_{\\text{mut}} = 0.01\\)  \n     - Repair iterations: 500  \n     - Tournament size: 5  \n   - **Initial restart**: Use provided parent solution if available; otherwise, random sampling.  \n   - **Adaptive mutation**: If sum is off by > 50% after repair, reduce mutation probability by 0.005.  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   - Dominant cost: \\(O(\\text{pop\\_size} \\times \\text{Generations} \\times n_{\\text{points}})\\)  \n   - Estimation: 500 generations \u00d7 100 pop \u00d7 400 \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\) \u2192 20e6 ops (feasible in 1300s).  \n   - Dynamic repair: Cease repair if > 10x max iterations.  \n   - Time checks: After crossover/mutation, check against budget (kill if exceeded).  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   - **Feasibility**: Ensure \\(h_i \\in [0, 1]\\) and sum \u2248 \\(n_{\\text{sum}}\\) (up to \\(\\epsilon = 10^{-10}\\)).  \n   - **Resiliency**: Return best solution even with noisy evaluation; fallback to parent for unavailable parent.  \n   - **Output**: `h_values` (array), \\(C_5\\) (float, scaled \\(10^{10}\\)), \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\).  \n   - **Verification**: Recompute \\(C_5\\) via full correlation (no derivation, per task).  \n   - **Fallback**: If no parent supplied, fallback to random-initialized solution.  \n   - **Scalability**: Handle \\(n_{\\text{points}}\\) changes; interpolate/downsample \\(h\\) via linear interpolation and reproject.",
